THE MULTIPLANETARY MESSENGER

Legitimacy, Human Mediation, and Institutional Cooperation Across Interplanetary Societies

Final Theoretical PhD Draft — Version 1.0

Principal Conceptor: Ouadi Maakoul
Research Collaboration: SI TEAM
Research Stage: Theoretical framework complete; empirical programme specified; pre-empirical
Date: November 2026

Revision note. This version consolidates v0.3 and responds to the targeted empirical and methodological check. Four corrections are made. First, the BBNJ Agreement is correctly presented: the adopted 2023 text provides for dispute settlement through UNCLOS Part XV procedures with opt-out declarations and an automatic carve-out for sovereignty and territorial disputes under Article 60(9); the International Marine Dispute Settlement Council and the Global Ocean Monitoring and Enforcement System are proposals advanced in the academic literature, not enacted treaty institutions. Second, the QCA design is reduced from six conditions to four core conditions (communication disruption, infrastructural dependence, institutional oversight, and local autonomy), eliminating the limited-diversity problem that six conditions imposed on a case universe of approximately ten to fifteen cases. Third, the Section 15 cross-reference is corrected; the systematic review protocol is stated in Section 14. Fourth, the August 2026 Antarctic Treaty consultative-status deadlock is incorporated as a crucial process-tracing case. Three substantive proposals are integrated. Initiation rights are operationalised through a minority-node trigger. The ratchet variable is defined against an enumerated mandate registry to eliminate post-hoc technical-necessity excuses. The Antarctic deadlock is used to demonstrate the mechanism by which consensus admission destroys throughput and output legitimacy. The draft remains pre-empirical and makes no claim of validation.

Abstract

The transition from a single-planet human civilisation toward a multiplanetary civilisation introduces an institutional problem that cannot be reduced to transportation, engineering, or life-support autonomy: how can human societies preserve the capacity to cooperate when they become geographically separated, develop divergent normative orders, and operate under communication delays that make continuous terrestrial oversight impossible?

This research investigates the interaction between four core conditions—communication disruption, infrastructural dependence, institutional oversight, and local autonomy—and their effects on the preservation or breakdown of interplanetary cooperation. It proposes a mediation function comprising a limited mandate with an enumerated competences registry, a selection and review procedure, a registry of represented and unrepresented interests, a layered dispute-classification architecture, an emergency constitution with a baseline-sensitive ratchet-detection mechanism, an adjudication referral pathway specified through three comparable institutional models, and a compliance architecture modelled on horizontal legal orders. The Messenger is one role inside this function, not its protagonist.

The central normative question is the constituent problem: a pre-autonomy institution cannot obtain consent from a population that does not yet exist, and state authorisation cannot legitimate an institution vis-à-vis a population that never chose to be governed by those states. The draft engages this problem through the 2026 Oxford Handbook of Constituent Power and distinguishes provisional legitimacy from informal authority and from trusteeship by a criterion of initiation rights: the governed must have standing to initiate reversal through a minority-node trigger, not merely to petition for it. The draft does not claim to solve the constituent problem. It claims to specify the conditions under which a provisional authority can be legitimate without solving it, and the conditions under which it cannot.

The contribution is a falsifiable comparative research programme. Its home discipline is political science, its core method is qualitative comparative analysis with process tracing, and its experimental components are subordinate. The novelty claim is conditional on the completion of the systematic review specified in Section 14. The draft specifies in advance what evidence would force revision and what evidence would force abandonment.

Keywords: multiplanetary governance; constituent power; provisional legitimacy; mediation; horizontal legal orders; infrastructural power; legal pluralism; qualitative comparative analysis; limited diversity; Antarctic Treaty System; preregistration.

