1. Introduction

1.1 From a Messenger to a mediation function, and from a function to a comparative programme

Version 0.1 asked who the Multiplanetary Messenger should be. Version 0.2 demoted the Messenger to one role inside a mediation function. Version 0.3 embedded the function in the constituent power literature and the compliance literature for horizontal legal orders. Version 1.0 retains that architecture but corrects four empirical and methodological vulnerabilities that a rigorous comparative politics committee would identify immediately.

The research question is:

Under what conditions do communication disruption, infrastructural dependence, institutional oversight, and local autonomy interact to preserve or destroy cooperation between geographically separated human communities, and what institutional forms of mediation, adjudication, and enforcement are legitimate under those conditions?

This question is comparative and falsifiable. It is not a question about finding the right person. It is a question about which configurations of institutional conditions are sufficient, necessary, or neither for cooperation continuity.

1.2 The two unresolved problems, restated with their literatures

The constituent problem. A pre-autonomy institution cannot obtain consent from future persons, and state authorisation cannot legitimate an institution vis-à-vis a population that did not authorise it. The 2026 Oxford Handbook of Constituent Power supplies the analytical vocabulary. Besson argues that the democratic deficit of international organisations resides in the "lack of constituent authorship of the people subject to their law," and that reform proposals suffer from two blind spots: they situate instituent powers "beyond the state" while neglecting that member-state publics are themselves re-instituted, and they overlook the representative dimension of multi-public participation. A multiplanetary mediation function will not be constituted by a single demos. It will be constituted, if at all, by multiple nested re-instituted publics—terrestrial publics, settler publics, corporate constituencies, and future persons who cannot yet be represented.

Patberg distinguishes secession from withdrawal and asks whether either can be understood as an act of constituent power. His conclusion is that constituent power can play a role in both, "but it does not provide a comprehensive theory of legitimacy for either." This is the honest position for the draft: exit and refusal are not self-legitimating. They require a theory of legitimacy that constituent power alone cannot supply.

The provisional legitimacy problem. Coutinho examines "the growing normative authority exercised by international organizations through informal governance mechanisms that operate without treaty ratification or formal consent," identifying democratic deficits, limited contestability, and technocratic concentration. The draft's provisional legitimacy is a species of this phenomenon, but it must be distinguished from it. Informal authority persists; provisional authority is designed to be superseded. The distinguishing criterion cannot be temporariness alone, because a colonial mandate was also temporary. It must be a criterion of initiation rights: the governed must have standing to initiate the reversal procedure, not merely to petition for it. A sunset clause without an initiation right is a paternalism with a calendar.

1.3 The four corrections in this version

The BBNJ correction. Section 3.2 of v0.3 cited the BBNJ Agreement's implementation architecture as including an International Marine Dispute Settlement Council and a Global Ocean Monitoring and Enforcement System. These are not in the adopted 2023 text. The BBNJ Agreement provides for dispute settlement through UNCLOS Part XV procedures, with declarations under Article 287 and optional limitations under Article 298 applying mutatis mutandis, and with an automatic carve-out under Article 60(9) for disputes concerning the legal status of an area as within national jurisdiction or sovereignty over continental or insular territory. The IMDSC, GOMES, and Regional ABNJ Governance Councils are proposals advanced by scholars in 2025–2026. The distinction matters because presenting a proposed reform as enacted treaty law undermines empirical credibility.

The QCA correction. v0.3 specified six binary or fuzzy conditions, generating a truth table of sixty-four possible configurations. A case universe of approximately ten to fifteen cases would leave over seventy-five per cent of rows as logical remainders, and the cases are not causally homogeneous: historical colonial charters had transport latency measured in months and zero real-time telemetry, whereas low-Earth-orbit and lunar infrastructure has near-zero latency but high infrastructural capture. Mixing them in one truth table violates QCA scope conditions. The design is reduced to four core conditions, yielding sixteen configurations, and the high-latency high-technology regime is modelled through agent-based simulation rather than QCA.

The cross-reference correction. The Abstract and Revision Note of v0.3 referred to a systematic review protocol in Section 15, but the document ended at Section 13. The protocol is stated in Section 14 of this version.

The Antarctic correction. The August 2026 Antarctic Treaty consultative-status deadlock is incorporated not as background but as a crucial process-tracing case. It demonstrates the mechanism by which consensus admission rules convert input legitimacy into a veto that degrades throughput and output legitimacy.

1.4 What the draft does not claim

The draft does not claim that a multiplanetary mediation institution exists or is likely to be created. It does not claim that the proposed competency instruments are validated. It does not claim to solve the constituent problem. It claims only that the conditions under which provisional authority can be legitimate without solving the constituent problem are specifiable, and that some of those conditions are testable.

