2. The Adjudication Pathway: Three Comparable Models

v0.2 said mediation must refer rights, constitutional, and sovereignty disputes to "an adjudication, arbitration, or political negotiation procedure." This was a placeholder. This version replaces it with a comparison of three institutional models, each specified by jurisdiction, appointment, applicable law, and legitimacy conditions.

2.1 The Deep Seabed Disputes Chamber model

The Seabed Disputes Chamber is a standing chamber within the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea with exclusive jurisdiction over several categories of disputes concerning activities in the Area. It may constitute an ad hoc chamber of three judges and may give advisory opinions at the request of the Assembly or Council of the International Seabed Authority. Its structural features are directly analogous to the interplanetary case: a treaty regime prohibiting sovereignty claims, a shared resource, disputes involving states, entities, and private contractors, and no centralised enforcement.

The Chamber model has two advantages. It is a standing body, which means it can develop expertise and consistency. It has compulsory jurisdiction over defined categories of disputes, which means referral does not require ad hoc consent. Its disadvantage is that it is embedded in a treaty regime with a defined membership; a multiplanetary tribunal would need to specify how non-parties are brought within jurisdiction.

2.2 The PCA Outer Space Rules model

The Permanent Court of Arbitration adopted Optional Rules for Arbitration of Disputes Relating to Outer Space Activities in 2011. The rules are open to states, international organisations, non-governmental organisations, corporations, and private entities. They allow parties to appoint arbitrators with space-technical expertise and provide procedural certainty.

The PCA model has one advantage and one disadvantage relative to the Chamber. The advantage is flexibility: it can accommodate a wide range of parties, including firms that operate settlements. The disadvantage is that the rules apply only when all parties consent, either by a prior arbitration clause or by a submission agreement after a dispute has arisen. Consent is the binding constraint. Under blackout conditions, obtaining consent may be impossible.

2.3 The Zhu–Haqq-Misra referendum model

Zhu and Haqq-Misra propose referendums as a mechanism for political decision-making and sovereignty adjudication on Mars, recommending "advance determination of the conditions under which a martian referendum would be recognized as a best practice for any agency seeking to establish a long-duration settlement on Mars". The referendum model is not adjudication in the judicial sense. It is a constituent procedure. Its advantage is legitimacy: a referendum is an act of the governed. Its disadvantage is feasibility under latency and under conditions of material dependence, where the electorate may be small, transient, or vulnerable to infrastructural coercion.

2.4 Specification

The mediation function's referral pathway must name which of these three models applies to which dispute class. A rights dispute is referred to a Chamber-type tribunal. A commercial or contractual dispute is referred to PCA-type arbitration. A sovereignty dispute is referred to a referendum-type constituent procedure, with pre-agreed conditions. The referral itself must be authorised in advance, so that the mediator does not require fresh consensus to refer.

