3. Enforcement: The Five-Layer Model and the BBNJ Distinction

3.1 Vidigal's five-layer model

Vidigal develops a five-layer model of compliance control in horizontal legal orders, ranging from voluntary internal compliance to institutionally authorised sanctions, and highlighting the interplay between internal state mechanisms, bilateral enforcement, collective responses, and institutional determinations. The model's key insight is that compliance in a horizontal order does not require a centralised enforcer. It requires a layered architecture in which each layer has different legitimacy conditions.

Under interplanetary latency, the layers are not equally available. Reputational enforcement (layers 1–2) operates through shared understandings and reciprocal expectations and may function under short delays. Collective responses (layer 3) require coordination among multiple parties and are slower. Institutional determinations (layer 4) require a standing body with interpretive authority. Authorised sanctions (layer 5) require a legitimacy that no interplanetary institution is likely to possess in the near term.

3.2 What the mediation function can and cannot enforce

The mediation function can enforce nothing by itself. It can trigger enforcement at layers 1–3 by publishing findings, registering non-compliance, and convening collective responses. It can refer to layer 4 when a dispute requires an institutional determination. It cannot authorise layer-5 sanctions. This is a limitation, not a failure. A mediation function that claimed sanctioning power would exceed its mandate and forfeit its legitimacy.

3.3 The BBNJ analogy, correctly stated

The adopted 2023 BBNJ Agreement regulates jurisdiction ratione materiae as covering all disputes concerning the interpretation or application of the Agreement. The only explicit limitation is Article 60(9), which carves out disputes pertaining to "the legal status of an area as within national jurisdiction" or "sovereignty or other rights over continental or insular land territory or a claim thereto," and extends the carve-out to disputes that "necessarily involve the concurrent consideration" of such matters. Declarations regarding forum selection under Article 287 of UNCLOS and optional jurisdictional limitations under Article 298 apply to dispute resolution under the BBNJ Agreement unless a new declaration is submitted. The Agreement recognises in Article 60(8) the priority of dispute settlement procedures found in other instruments.

The International Marine Dispute Settlement Council and the Global Ocean Monitoring and Enforcement System are proposals advanced in the 2025–2026 literature, not enacted treaty institutions. The distinction is important for the framework: the BBNJ Agreement as adopted relies on consent-based dispute settlement with opt-outs and an automatic sovereignty carve-out, not on binding adjudication with centralised enforcement. The analogy for the interplanetary case is therefore not that a centralised enforcement architecture exists, but that a horizontal order can function through layered compliance mechanisms without one. The proposals for an IMDSC and GOMES are instructive as design options, not as evidence of what states have agreed to.

