5. Political Economy: Infrastructural Power

Material dependence is not an index of self-sufficiency. It is a structure of control. Berry introduces five constructs directly relevant to the interplanetary case: commercial orbital sovereignty, infrastructure dependency capture, orbital access stratification, corporate astropolitics, and the Orbital Social Contract with its pathological counter-form, orbital feudalism.

Infrastructure dependency capture is the condition in which a private operator controls the infrastructure on which a population depends, and thereby exercises sovereignty-like power without formal title. This is the structural condition of any settlement whose air, water, power, or communications are supplied by a single operator. A mediation function that treats material dependence as an index to be measured will miss the mechanism. The mechanism is control over the conditions of survival, and it translates directly into bargaining power regardless of formal autonomy.

5.1 Measurement model

Construct Indicator
Infrastructure dependency capture Share of life-support, power, and communications controlled by a single operator; number of alternative suppliers; contractual exit costs
Commercial orbital sovereignty Formal authority exercised by the operator over settlement governance; dispute-resolution clauses in operator contracts
Orbital access stratification Differential access to transport, bandwidth, and resupply across settlements and populations
Corporate astropolitics Operator participation in political negotiation; lobbying presence; personnel seconded to governance roles

These are measurable through contractual analysis, infrastructure mapping, and structured interviews. They are not reducible to a single index, and they should not be.

5.2 The distributive dimension

The new space economy is top-heavy: a small number of firms and states control the capital-intensive infrastructure. This structure creates intra-national equity issues and global equity dilemmas. A mediation function that ignores these structures will reproduce them. The framework includes a distributional audit of any mediated agreement: who bears the costs, who captures the benefits, and whether the agreement entrenches existing asymmetries.

