6. Legal Pluralism: From Principle to Interaction Regime

Bochmann shows how legal orders are established in spaces where state law is absent, using refugee camps as a case, and finds that what appears as pluralism dissolves into a powerful local camp law. A Mars settlement will not be a space of legal vacuum. It will be a space of local law, generated by the settlement's own practices, and that local law may be more powerful than the formal law of the flag state.

The distinction between weak and strong legal pluralism is the relevant analytical tool. Weak pluralism treats non-state orders as normative but not legal, and asks how the state recognises them. Strong pluralism treats them as law regardless of state recognition, and asks how multiple legal orders interact. The draft adopts strong pluralism for analytical purposes. The conflicts rule is therefore not a choice-of-law rule but an interaction regime with four mechanisms: recognition, coordination, contestation, and revision.

Recognition registers a local normative order as a competent interlocutor without necessarily treating it as authoritative over all matters. Coordination identifies overlaps and conflicts between orders and negotiates interim rules. Contestation allows a party to challenge the applicability of an order, with reasons published. Revision allows the interaction regime itself to be amended as local orders evolve.

