7. Emergency Powers: The Ratchet Variable and the Enumerated Baseline

7.1 The ratchet effect

Gross and Ní Aoláin identify the normalisation of the exception as the central risk: "What were deemed exceptional emergency actions in the past may now come to be regarded as normal, routine, and ordinary, in light of more recent and more dramatic emergency powers". The ratchet effect is the tendency of the baseline of normal powers to drift upward after each emergency. A sunset clause reverses the emergency powers but does not necessarily reverse the baseline shift. A protocol that reverses emergency powers but retains a broadened definition of "emergency" has ratcheted.

7.2 The endogeneity problem and its solution

The v0.3 ratchet metric R_t = (N_{t+1} - N_t) / E_t treats N_t, the scope of normal powers, as an objective scalar. It is not. During a solar flare or life-support failure, an operator will claim that expanded authority is merely technical implementation of existing survival-layer rules rather than an expansion of normal powers. The metric is therefore endogenous to the actor's own characterisation.

The solution is to define N_t strictly through enumerated jurisdictional competences in the Mandate Registry. Any power exercised during a blackout that was not pre-enumerated in N_t is scored as emergency power E_t. If that power is retained post-blackout without a ratified mandate amendment, the ratchet variable is positive by definition. Post-hoc technical-necessity excuses are eliminated because the baseline is the registry, not the actor's self-description.

Formally, let M_t be the set of enumerated competences in the Mandate Registry at time t, and let P_t be the set of powers actually exercised during the emergency. Emergency powers are E_t = P_t \setminus M_t. After reversal, the retained powers are P_{t+1} \cap P_t. The ratchet indicator is:

\rho_t = \frac{|(P_{t+1} \cap P_t) \setminus M_t|}{|E_t|}

where \rho_t = 0 indicates full reversal and \rho_t > 0 indicates ratcheting. H2 tests whether protocols with an enumerated registry and an automatic reversal trigger produce \rho_t significantly lower than protocols with discretionary emergency powers.

