10. Initiation Rights: Operationalising Provisional Legitimacy Against Capture

The distinction between provisional legitimacy and trusteeship via initiation rights is the draft's strongest normative contribution. But under infrastructure dependency capture, an operator who controls oxygen, water, and bandwidth can suppress the coordination required for a population to exercise an initiation right. If initiation requires a supermajority or synchronous cross-settlement consensus, the operator can wait out the petition or fragment the coalition.

10.1 The minority-node trigger

Initiation rights cannot require a supermajority or synchronous consensus to trigger a review. The design borrows from comparative referendum mechanics, where citizen-initiated referendums are triggered by a petition and where political minorities can exercise power over the initiation process. The proposal is a minority-node trigger: if a low threshold—for example, fifteen per cent of registered inhabitants in any single habitation module—logs a reversal petition into the shared registry, the review clock starts automatically, and the burden of justification shifts onto the provisional authority.

10.2 Why the threshold is low and node-specific

The threshold is low because the purpose of the initiation right is to overcome coordination barriers, not to decide the outcome. The petition does not reverse the authority; it starts a review. A low threshold reduces the cost of collective action and makes the right exercisable even when the operator controls communications. The trigger is node-specific because a single habitation module is a bounded population with a shared infrastructure dependency; a petition from one module cannot be ignored as a minority faction if the module is a distinct affected constituency.

10.3 Legitimacy condition

The minority-node trigger satisfies the initiation-rights criterion because it gives the governed standing to start a review without requiring permission from the authority being reviewed. A sunset clause without this trigger is a paternalism with a calendar. A sunset clause with this trigger is a provisional authority with a built-in reversal mechanism.

