14. Systematic Review Protocol (Condition of the Novelty Claim)

The novelty claim is conditional on a systematic review, which is a prerequisite for any empirical work. The review covers global governance legitimacy, constituent power and international institutions, intercultural competence and mediator assessment, Track II diplomacy, negotiation under communication delay, polycentric governance including the Ostrom Workshop's space governance project, Mars political autonomy and referendum proposals, international space law including the Outer Space Treaty, the Artemis Accords, the Moon Agreement, and the contested interpretation of Articles I and VI, Antarctic governance including the consultative-status mechanism, colonial communication systems, corporate and charter-city governance, federalism and commons governance, and AI-assisted mediation and responsibility attribution.

The review is reported with search strings, databases, inclusion and exclusion criteria, quality assessment tools, synthesis method, and a PRISMA-style flow diagram. It is published before QCA or experiments are conducted, so that the hypotheses can be revised in light of what is already established. The review includes a dedicated section comparing the gradualist mediation-function model with the Zhu–Haqq-Misra referendum model, specifying the conditions under which each is preferable.

---

15. Limitations

This version remains theoretical. The QCA has not been conducted. The experiments have not been run. The systematic review has not been completed. The adjudication models are compared on structural features, not on performance under latency. The enforcement model is borrowed from international law and has not been tested in an interplanetary setting. The ratchet variable is a proposal, not a finding. The engagement with the rival thesis specifies conditions of comparison but does not adjudicate between the models. The Antarctic deadlock is used as a crucial case, but a single case cannot establish a general mechanism. The minority-node trigger is a design proposal, not an empirically validated mechanism.

The draft's claim is modest: that the mediation function can be specified with enough precision to be compared, tested, and falsified, and that the constituent problem can be bracketed without being ignored. Whether that claim survives the systematic review and the comparative analysis is an open question.

---

16. Conclusion: A Comparative Research Programme

The development of a multiplanetary civilisation introduces a problem that is simultaneously institutional, political, and normative. The physical separation of human communities may produce divergent normative orders, infrastructural dependencies, and bargaining positions. This separation does not imply hostility, but neither does technological interconnection guarantee cooperation.

v0.1 asked who the Messenger should be. v0.2 asked what a mediation function would look like. v0.3 embedded the function in the constituent power and compliance literatures. v1.0 asks a comparative question: which configurations of communication disruption, infrastructural dependence, institutional oversight, and local autonomy are sufficient for cooperation continuity, and which institutional forms of mediation, adjudication, and enforcement are legitimate under those configurations?

The answer is not a hero. It is a configuration. The mediation function is one component. The adjudication pathway is another. The enforcement architecture is a third. The constituent problem is the normative horizon against which all three are assessed. The framework does not solve the constituent problem. It specifies the conditions under which provisional authority can be legitimate without solving it—through initiation rights, a minority-node trigger, an enumerated mandate registry, and a layered compliance architecture—and the conditions under which it cannot.

The research programme is offered with explicit conditions for revision and abandonment. If the systematic review reveals that the contribution is already established, the novelty claim will be withdrawn. If QCA fails to produce a stable model, the configurational claim will be abandoned. If the constituent problem proves intractable, the normative component will be reduced. These are not concessions. They are the conditions under which a theoretical claim becomes a scientific one.

