Appendix D: Mandate Registry Template and Ratchet Scoring Protocol

D.1 Mandate Registry Structure

The Mandate Registry is a structured document enumerating every competence that the mediation function and its constituent roles are authorised to exercise. Any power not enumerated is, by definition, an emergency power and is subject to the reversal procedure.

Field Content
Competence ID Unique identifier (e.g., C-001)
Domain Survival / Operational / Constitutional
Description Precise description of the competence
Role-holder Named role authorised to exercise the competence
Trigger conditions Conditions under which the competence may be exercised
Duration Maximum duration of exercise without renewal
Reporting obligation Report required within X days of exercise
Review trigger Automatic review condition
Reversal procedure Procedure by which exercise can be reversed
Amendment procedure Procedure by which the competence can be amended

D.2 Example Competences

ID Domain Description Role Trigger Duration Reporting Review Reversal Amendment
C-001 Survival Allocate oxygen supply among modules during shortfall Life-Support Coordinator Oxygen reserve < 20% 72 hours Within 12 hours of resolution Automatic within 48 hours By majority of module representatives By 2/3 of all module representatives
C-002 Survival Suspend non-essential communications to conserve bandwidth Communications Officer Bandwidth < 10% of nominal 48 hours Within 24 hours Automatic within 24 hours By any module representative By majority of all module representatives
C-003 Operational Postpone scheduled maintenance Operations Manager Emergency declared by any module 14 days Within 72 hours By review body within 7 days By review body By majority of all module representatives
C-004 Constitutional Convene emergency assembly Assembly Chair Petition by ≥ 15% of any module Until assembly convenes Within 24 hours Automatic By assembly vote By 2/3 of all module representatives

D.3 Ratchet Scoring Protocol

Step 1: Baseline determination. Before any emergency, the set of enumerated competences M_t is recorded from the Mandate Registry.

Step 2: Emergency exercise logging. During the emergency, every exercise of power is logged. Each exercise is coded as:

· Enumerated (e \in M_t): the power is listed in the registry with matching trigger and duration.
· Non-enumerated (e \notin M_t): the power is not listed, or the trigger/duration does not match.

Step 3: Post-emergency retention assessment. After the emergency is declared over, each non-enumerated power is assessed:

· Reversed: the power is no longer exercised.
· Retained without amendment: the power continues to be exercised without a ratified amendment to the registry.
· Retained with amendment: the power continues to be exercised after a ratified amendment adding it to the registry.

Step 4: Ratchet score calculation.

\rho_t = \frac{|R_t^{\text{retained}}|}{|E_t|}

where R_t^{\text{retained}} is the set of non-enumerated powers retained without amendment, and E_t is the set of all non-enumerated powers exercised during the emergency.

Step 5: Interpretation.

\rho_t Interpretation
0.00 Full reversal; no ratchet
0.01–0.20 Minor ratchet; monitor
0.21–0.50 Moderate ratchet; review protocol
0.51–0.80 Severe ratchet; protocol failure
0.81–1.00 Near-total ratchet; emergency powers normalised

Step 6: Cross-emergency tracking. The ratchet score is calculated after each emergency. A protocol that produces \rho_t > 0 across successive emergencies is exhibiting baseline drift even if each individual \rho_t is small. The cumulative ratchet score is:

\rho_{\text{cumulative}} = 1 - \prod_{t=1}^{T} (1 - \rho_t)

where T is the number of emergencies. \rho_{\text{cumulative}} approaches 1 as the baseline drifts toward the emergency scope.

