Appendix E: Minority-Node Trigger Specification

E.1 Purpose

The minority-node trigger operationalises the initiation right that distinguishes provisional legitimacy from trusteeship. It allows a bounded population within a single habitation module to initiate a review of provisional authority without requiring supermajority or cross-settlement consensus.

E.2 Trigger Conditions

Parameter Specification Justification
Threshold 15% of registered inhabitants in any single habitation module Low enough to overcome coordination barriers; high enough to prevent frivolous petitions
Registration Petitioners must be registered inhabitants of the module for ≥ 30 days Prevents transient manipulation
Petition format Written petition logged in shared registry with petitioner identifiers Transparency and auditability
Review clock Starts automatically upon threshold met Burden of justification shifts to authority
Review body Independent review body (or, if none, ad hoc panel selected by lot from all modules) Independence from authority being reviewed
Burden of proof Authority must demonstrate that the challenged power is enumerated in the Mandate Registry or justified by emergency conditions Reverses the default: authority must justify, not petitioner
Outcome Review body issues finding within 30 days; finding is published Timely and transparent
Appeal Either party may appeal to adjudication pathway (Section 2) Integration with broader architecture

E.3 Procedural Rules

1. Logging: The petition is logged in the shared registry within 24 hours of submission. The registry is accessible to all modules.
2. Notification: The authority being challenged is notified within 24 hours of logging.
3. Response: The authority may submit a written response within 7 days.
4. Review: The review body convenes within 14 days of logging.
5. Finding: The review body issues a written finding within 30 days of convening. The finding specifies whether the challenged power is (a) enumerated, (b) non-enumerated but justified by emergency, or (c) non-enumerated and not justified.
6. Consequence: If (c), the power is reversed and the authority must amend the registry if it wishes to exercise it in future. If (b), the power is retained for the duration of the declared emergency but is subject to the ratchet scoring protocol (Appendix D).
7. Publication: All findings are published in the shared registry and are accessible to all modules and to the adjudication pathway.

E.4 Anti-Capture Safeguards

· Communication independence: Petitions may be logged through any communication channel, including low-bandwidth channels that the operator does not control. If the operator controls all channels, the petition may be logged through a pre-positioned physical registry (e.g., a sealed box in each module) that is opened by the review body.
· Anonymity protection: Petitioner identities are visible to the review body but may be redacted in public findings if petitioners request anonymity and the review body determines that disclosure would expose them to retaliation.
· Quorum protection: If the review body cannot convene because of communication disruption, the review clock is suspended but not cancelled; the review must convene within 14 days of restored communication.

