Appendix K: Agent-Based Model Specification (for H3)

K.1 Purpose

The ABM explores configurations that QCA cannot observe: high-latency, high-technology interplanetary regimes with multiple settlements, infrastructural dependencies, and variable oversight. It is not a test of the framework but a tool for exploring its implications under conditions where no empirical cases exist.

K.2 Model Structure

Agents:

· Settlements (N = 3–10): each with a population, an infrastructure endowment, a local authority, and a communication capability.
· Operators (N = 1–3): each controlling a share of life-support, power, or communications infrastructure across settlements.
· Mediation function (N = 1): a registry, a forum, a disclosure protocol, and a review body.

Environment:

· Communication latency L (minutes, one-way).
· Blackout frequency B (days per year).
· Resource scarcity S (0–1).

Interaction rules:

· Settlements exchange proposals through the mediation function's registry.
· Proposals are subject to response windows (Appendix F).
· Non-response triggers fallback procedures.
· Disputes are classified (Appendix G) and referred or mediated.
· Operators may exercise infrastructural power (e.g., restrict supply) to influence outcomes.

K.3 Parameters and Sweep

Parameter Range Steps
L 3–22 minutes 5 values
B 0–180 days/year 5 values
S 0.1–0.9 5 values
Oversight strength O_v 0–1 5 values
Local autonomy A 0–1 5 values
Number of settlements 3–10 3 values
Number of operators 1–3 3 values

Total parameter combinations: 5 \times 5 \times 5 \times 5 \times 5 \times 3 \times 3 = 28{,}125. Each combination run 10 times with different random seeds. Total runs: 281,250.

K.4 Outcomes

· Cooperation continuity: proportion of runs without breakdown event.
· Ratchet score: \rho_t (Appendix D).
· Distributional inequality: Gini coefficient of resource access across settlements.
· Infrastructural capture: proportion of runs in which a single operator controls > 80% of a critical infrastructure domain.

K.5 Convergence and Validation

· Convergence: Monte Carlo standard error < 0.01 for all outcomes.
· Validation: face validity assessed by domain experts; sensitivity analysis on all parameters; comparison of simulated outcomes to QCA predictions where cases overlap.
· Limitations: The ABM is a stylised model, not a predictive tool. Its purpose is to explore logical implications, not to forecast.

K.6 Software

Model implemented in Python using Mesa (agent-based modelling framework). Code deposited in GitHub repository upon publication. Documentation follows the ODD (Overview, Design concepts, Details) protocol.

