Appendix L: Glossary of Terms

Term Definition
Adjudication pathway The specified procedure by which disputes that exceed mediation mandate are referred to a tribunal, arbitration, or constituent procedure.
Blackout A period during which communication between parties is confirmed lost for reasons outside the parties' control.
Constituent power The authority to constitute a political order; the power to found or refound institutions.
Cooperation continuity The absence of breakdown indicators over a defined period; operationalised through six indicators (v0.2 §7.4).
Emergency constitution The set of rules specifying who may act during an emergency, for how long, under what reporting obligation, with what review trigger, and with what reversal mechanism.
Enumerated competences The set of powers listed in the Mandate Registry; any power not listed is, by definition, an emergency power.
Infrastructural dependence The condition in which a population depends on infrastructure controlled by a single operator for survival or communication.
Initiation right The right of the governed to initiate a review of provisional authority without requiring permission from the authority being reviewed.
Institutional oversight The composite of accountability mechanisms and monitoring capacity.
Legal pluralism (strong) The analytical position that non-state normative orders are law regardless of state recognition.
Legal pluralism (weak) The analytical position that non-state normative orders are normative but not legal, and are recognised (or not) by the state.
Limited diversity In QCA, the condition in which many truth-table rows have no empirical cases, producing logical remainders.
Mandate Registry The structured document enumerating every competence authorised to the mediation function and its roles.
Minority-node trigger A procedure by which a low threshold of inhabitants in a single habitation module can initiate a review of provisional authority.
Polycentric governance A system of governance with multiple, partly overlapping centres that coordinate, monitor, sanction, and learn.
Provisional legitimacy The legitimacy of an authority that is explicitly temporary, reviewable, and designed to be superseded, and that is subject to an initiation right.
Ratchet score (\rho_t) The proportion of non-enumerated emergency powers retained without amendment after an emergency.
Silence protocol The set of rules assigning defined meanings to silence under latency: procedural delay, non-response, suspended response, or deliberate withholding.
Truth table In QCA, a table listing all logically possible combinations of conditions and the outcome for each combination.

